Hey Labib,

This worked!

I currently have a product that converts the voltages of modern camera
batteries (14.4v Li-ion) for older 16mm Motion Picture Film cameras.
Depending on the model the enclosure varies and has either a 12v or 24v
requirement.

So far I have been using these boards from Amazon/Aliexpress
https://shorturl.at/xznXI

I have been asked to make these for a more advanced, high-speed version of
these cameras. However, the higher amperage draws push the board's limits,
causing overheating without a cooling system (which I can not use a fan
system due to noise). Because shooting film at higher frame rates limits
the total recording time at these higher amperages to only a few minutes at
absolute most, these boards will most likely still work for me.

But I think it would be worth it to have a proprietary board made up with a
bit of extra overhead and the potential to work with 35mm Camera systems as
well plus some additional features.

I've attached a spec sheet I've created chatting back and forth with
Claude, so hopefully it makes sense to you.

As for the footprint of the board I don't have much room to get any larger,
Unless there was possibility of doing an L shape. Having a threaded input
terminal on the board for the brass ring terminals in the picture could
potentially clear up some room.

There are some large magnets on the back side of the enclosure for its
functionality with the Camera which could potentially be used as an
additional heat sink.

I am also open to splitting it into two separate versions instead of using
a single Buck/Boost board, if that can substantially decrease the
footprint.


Thanks!

Thomas
